1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,592
Resist the folklore fix. Gate p95 is high;
someone proposes “rewrite in WebFlux”
before reading a single span.

2
00:00:08,711 --> 00:00:21,071
Performance tuning in a Spring harbor
stack starts from signals: RED metrics on
releaseGate, saturation gauges on pools,
traces for critical path, and — when CPU
is the villain — a flame graph that names
the hot method.

3
00:00:21,192 --> 00:00:31,631
Triage in order. Is it error rate or
latency? If latency, is it time in gate
code, time in Feign to billing, or time in
billing SQL?

4
00:00:31,811 --> 00:00:42,780
Micrometer HTTP timers and your
harbor.gate.release.duration split edge
versus business method. Traces confirm.
Only then profile.

5
00:00:42,899 --> 00:00:56,099
Skipping steps is how teams spend a week
on reactive rewrites while billing’s
connection pool was the whole story. Look
at this text for a moment. Here the flame
is not a Java hot loop — it is waiting on
billing.

6
00:00:56,219 --> 00:01:03,228
The fix might be caching non-hazard
quotes, batching, or raising billing
capacity — not rewriting gate’s
controllers.

7
00:01:03,347 --> 00:01:12,852
A different flame that shows
TariffCalculator.quote burning CPU on
every request might mean an accidental
O(n²) surcharge table scan.

8
00:01:12,971 --> 00:01:25,787
Socket wait versus CPU burn demand
opposite remedies; the profile
distinguishes them when metrics only say
“slow.” Look at this java for a moment.
Walk the before/after with numbers.

9
00:01:25,907 --> 00:01:36,395
Six containers at 80ms each is ~480ms of
Feign alone on the critical path; one bulk
quote at 120ms changes the booth math
without touching WebFlux.

10
00:01:36,515 --> 00:01:49,331
If the contract cannot bulk yet, parallel
calls with a bounded executor help — and
need a bulkhead so you do not stampede
billing. Measure again on the same Grafana
panels; anecdotes are not acceptance
criteria.

11
00:01:49,451 --> 00:02:02,531
Pool tuning follows gauges, not vibes:
Look at this example. spring: If
hikaricp_connections_pending climbs while
CPU is idle, enlarge carefully or shorten
queries — do not guess.

12
00:02:02,652 --> 00:02:10,187
Feign timeouts that exceed user patience
keep threads stuck; timeouts that are far
below billing p99 manufacture errors.

13
00:02:10,307 --> 00:02:19,692
Load-test after each change with a
realistic check-in mix — multi-container
trucks, hazard classes, cold caches — and
watch the same Grafana panels that page
you.

14
00:02:19,811 --> 00:02:24,419
A laptop profile with an empty billing
stub will not show socket wait.

15
00:02:24,539 --> 00:02:33,659
Failure modes of folklore tuning: more
replicas that each still fan out N Feign
calls; JVM flag churn before fixing N+1 or
unbounded retries;

16
00:02:33,780 --> 00:02:42,372
caching without eviction that trades
latency for the memory incident in the
next episode; “optimize Jackson” when 58%
of the flame is socket wait.

17
00:02:42,491 --> 00:02:56,051
Trade-offs: caching tariff quotes helps
non-hazard traffic and risks stale rates
for IMDG changes — key by hazard and
version the tariff table. Vertical scaling
buys time; chatty APIs return when traffic
multiplies.

18
00:02:56,171 --> 00:03:06,972
Prefer eliminating round trips over micro-
optimizing serializers. Document each
change with before/after p95 on the same
panel and the same load-test script.

19
00:03:07,091 --> 00:03:19,859
If you cannot show the number, you did not
finish the tuning — you only shipped a
hypothesis. Rollback is part of tuning: a
bulk endpoint that regresses billing CPU
should revert while you redesign the
query.

20
00:03:19,979 --> 00:03:32,483
A misconception is equating “more
replicas” with a fix when each replica
still makes the same chatty Feign fan-out.
Another is tuning JVM flags before fixing
an N+1 or unbounded retry storm.

21
00:03:32,603 --> 00:03:38,195
A third is trusting a local laptop profile
when production waits on a different
billing region.

22
00:03:38,315 --> 00:03:46,715
When the bad signal is heap growth, GC
thrash, or native memory, the lens narrows
further — memory optimization with dumps
and cache bounds.
